 to iterate over the activity records in a buffer. A buffer of activity records is typically obtained by receiving a CUpti_BuffersCallbackCompleteFunc callback. Stop iterating the buffer when an error occurs.\nL6785: An example of typical usage:\nL6786: \nL6787:     CUpti_Activity *record = NULL;\nL6788:     CUptiResult status = CUPTI_SUCCESS;\nL6789:       do {\nL6790:          status = cuptiActivityGetNextRecord(buffer, validSize, &record);\nL6791:          if(status == CUPTI_SUCCESS) {\nL6792:               // Use record here...\nL6793:          }\nL6794:          else if (status == CUPTI_ERROR_MAX_LIMIT_REACHED)\nL6795:              break;\nL6796:          else if (status == CUPTI_ERROR_INVALID_KIND)\nL6797:              break;\nL6798:          else {\nL6799:              goto Error;\nL6800:          }\nL6801:        } while (1);\nL6802: Parameters:\nL6803: \nL6804: \nL6805:   * buffer – The buffer containing activity records\nL6806: \nL6807:   * record – Inputs the previous record returned by cuptiActivityGetNextRecord and returns the next activity record from the buffer. If input value is NULL, returns the first activity record in the buffer. Records of certain kinds like CUPTI_ACTIVITY_KIND_CONCURRENT_KERNEL may contain invalid (0) timestamps, indicating that no timing information could be collected for lack of device memory.\nL6808:   * validBufferSizeBytes – The number of valid bytes in the buffer.\nL6809: \nL6810: Return values:\nL6811: \nL6812: \nL6813:   * CUPTI_SUCCESS –\nL6814: \nL6815:   * CUPTI_ERROR_NOT_INITIALIZED –\nL6816: \nL6817:   * CUPTI_ERROR_MAX_LIMIT_REACHED – if no more records in the buffer\nL6818: \nL6819:   * CUPTI_ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER – if `buffer` is NULL.\nL6820: \nL6821:   * CUPTI_ERROR_INVALID_KIND – if activity record is either incomplete or invalid\nL6822: \nL6823: 【1388†CUptiResult】 cuptiActivityGetNumDroppedRecords(\nL6824: \nL6825:     CUcontext context,\nL6826:     uint32_t streamId,\nL6827:     size_t *dropped,\nL6828: )【1421†#】\nL6829: Get the number of activity records that were dropped of insufficient buffer space.\nL6830: Get the number of records that were dropped because of insufficient buffer space. The dropped count includes records that could not be recorded because CUPTI did not have activity buffer space available for the record (because the CUpti_BuffersCallbackRequestFunc callback did not return an empty buffer of sufficient size) and also CDP records that could not be record because the device-size buffer was full (size is controlled by the CUPTI_ACTIVITY_ATTR_DEVICE_BUFFER_SIZE_CDP attribute).\nL6831: The dropped count maintained for the queue is reset to zero when this function is called.\nL6832: Parameters:\nL6833: \nL6834: \nL6835:   * context – The context, or NULL to get dropped count from global queue\nL6836: \nL6837:   * streamId – The stream ID\nL6838: \nL6839:   * dropped – The number of records that were dropped since the last call to this function.\nL6840: \nL6841: Return values:\nL6842: \nL6843: \nL6844:   * CUPTI_SUCCESS –\nL6845: \nL6846:   * CUPTI_ERROR_NOT_INITIALIZED –\nL6847: \nL6848:   * CUPTI_ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER – if `dropped` is NULL\nL6849: \nL6850: 【1388†CUptiResult】 cuptiActivityGetStructSize(\nL6851: \nL6852:     【811†CUpti_ActivityKind】 activityKind,\nL6853:     uint32_t version,\nL6854:     size_t *activityStructSize,\nL6855: )【1422†#】\nL6856: Get the size of the activity struct for a given CUPTI version.\nL6857: \nL6858: This API is applicable only for predefined activity structs.\nL6859: \nL6860: Parameters:\nL6861: \nL6862: \nL6863:   * activityKind – The activity kind to get the size for.\nL6864: \nL6865:   * activityStructSize – The size of the activity struct.\nL6866:   * version – The version of the CUPTI API. If 0, returns size for current version. If non-zero, returns size for the specified version. The version format is xxyyzz, e.g., 130000 for CUPTI 13.0, 130100 for CUPTI 13.1. The version of CUPTI can be queried using cuptiGetVersion API. Only versions >= 130000 are supported.\nL6867: \nL6868: Return values:\nL6869: \nL6870: \nL6871:   * CUPTI_SUCCESS –\nL6872: \nL6873:   * CUPTI_ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER – if `activityStructSize` is NULL\nL6874: \nL6875:   * CUPTI_ERROR_INVALID_KIND – if `activityKind` is invalid.\nL6876:   * CUPTI_ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED – if `version` is non-zero and is less than 130000.\nL6877: \nL6878: 【1388†CUptiResult】 cuptiActivityPopExternalCorrelationId(\nL6879: \nL6880:     【1303†CUpti_ExternalCorrelationKind】 kind,\nL6881:     uint64_t *lastId,\nL6882: )【1423†#】\nL6883: \nL6884: \nL6885: Pop an external correlation id for the calling thread.\nL6886: \nL6887: This function notifies CUPTI that the calling thread is leaving an external API region.\nL6888: \nL6889: Parameters:\nL6890: \nL6891: \nL6892:   * kind – The kind of external API activities should be correlated with.\nL6893:   * lastId – If the function returns successful, contains the last external correlation id for this `kind`, can be NULL.\nL6894: \nL6895: Return values:\nL6896: \nL6897: \nL6898:   * CUPTI_SUCCESS –\nL6899: \nL6900:   * CUPTI_ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER – The external API kind is invalid.\nL6901: \nL6902:   * CUPTI_ERROR_QUEUE_EMPTY – No external id is currently associated with `kind`.\nL6903: \nL6904: 【1388†CUptiResult】 cuptiActivityPushExternalCorrelationId(\nL6905: \nL6906:     【1303†CUpti_ExternalCorrelationKind】 kind,\nL6907:     uint64_t id,\nL6908: )【1424†#】\nL6909: Push an external correlation id for the calling thread.\nL6910: \nL6911: This function notifies CUPTI that the calling thread is entering an external API region. When a CUPTI activity API record is created while within an external API region and CUPTI_ACTIVITY_KIND_EXTERNAL_CORRELATION is enabled, the activity API record will be preceded by a 【270†CUpti_ActivityExternalCorrelation】 record for each 【401†CUpti_ExternalCorrelationKind】.\nL6912: \nL6913: Parameters:\nL6914:   * kind – The kind of external API activities should be correlated with.\nL6915: \nL6916:   * id – External correlation id.\nL6917: \nL6918: Return values:\nL6919: \nL6920: \nL6921:   * CUPTI_SUCCESS –\nL6922: \nL6923:   * CUPTI_ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER – The external API kind is invalid\nL6924: \nL6925: 【1388†CUptiResult】 cuptiActivityRegisterCallbacks(\nL6926: \nL6927:     【1425†CUpti_BuffersCallbackRequestFunc】 funcBufferRequested,\nL6928:     【1426†CUpti_BuffersCallbackCompleteFunc】 funcBufferCompleted,\nL6929: )【1427†#】\nL6930: \nL6931: \nL6932: Registers callback functions with CUPTI for activity buffer handling.\nL7405: 【1388†CUptiResult】 cuptiGetTimestamp(uint64_t *timestamp)【1450†#】\nL7406: \nL7407: \nL7408: Get the CUPTI timestamp.\nL7409: \nL7410: Returns a timestamp normalized to correspond with the start and end timestamps reported in the CUPTI activity records. The timestamp is reported in nanoseconds.\nL7411: \nL7412: Parameters:\nL7413: \nL7414: \nL7415: timestamp – Returns the CUPTI timestamp\nL7416: \nL7417: Return values:\nL7418: \nL7419: \nL7420:   * CUPTI_SUCCESS –\nL7421: \nL7422:   * CUPTI_ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER – if `timestamp` is NULL\nL7423: \nL7424: 【1388†CUptiResult】 cuptiIsTracingSessionRunning(uint8_t *isRunning)【1451†#】\nL7425: Check whether a CUPTI tracing session is still running.\nL7426: \nL7427: Can be used to determine if it is safe to unload your CUPTI based tool.\nL7428: \nL7429: Parameters:\nL7430: \nL7431: \nL7432: isRunning – Returns whether the tracing session is still running.\nL7433: \nL7434: Return values:\nL7435: \nL7436: \nL7437:   * CUPTI_SUCCESS –\nL7438: \nL7439:   * CUPTI_ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER – if `isRunning` is NULL.\nL7440: \nL7441:   * CUPTI_ERROR_UNKNOWN – if an unknown error occurred.\nL7442: \nL7443: 【1388†CUptiResult】 cuptiSetThreadIdType(【1195†CUpti_ActivityThreadIdType】 type)【1452†#】\nL7444: \nL7445: \nL7446: Set the thread-id type.\nL7447: CUPTI uses the method corresponding to set type to generate the thread-id. See enum 【386†CUpti_ActivityThreadIdType】 for the list of methods. Activity records having thread-id field contain the same value. Thread id type must not be changed during the profiling session to avoid thread-id value mismatch across activity records.\nL7448: \nL7449: Return values:\nL7450: \nL7451: \nL7452:   * CUPTI_SUCCESS –\nL7453: \nL7454:   * CUPTI_ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED – if `type` is not supported on the platform\nL7455: ## 6.1.9. Typedefs【1453†#】\nL7456: \nL7457: typedef void (*CUpti_BuffersCallbackCompleteFunc)(CUcontext context, uint32_t streamId, uint8_t *buffer, size_t size, size_t validSize)【1426†#】\nL7458: \nL7459: \nL7460: Function type for callback used by CUPTI to return a buffer of activity records.\nL7461: This callback function returns to the CUPTI client a buffer containing activity records. The buffer contains `validSize` bytes of activity records which should be read using cuptiActivityGetNextRecord. The number of dropped records can be read using cuptiActivityGetNumDroppedRecords. After this call CUPTI relinquished ownership of the buffer and will not use it anymore. The client may return the buffer to CUPTI using the CUpti_BuffersCallbackRequestFunc callback.\nL7462: Note: CUDA 6.0 onwards, all buffers returned by this callback are global buffers i.e. there is no context/stream specific buffer. User needs to parse the global buffer to extract the context/stream specific activity records.\nL7463: Param context:\nL7464: \nL7465: \nL7466: The context this buffer is associated with. If NULL, the buffer is associated with the global activities. This field is deprecated as of CUDA 6.0 and will always be NULL.\nL7467: \nL7468: Param streamId:\nL7469: \nL7470: \nL7471: The stream id this buffer is associated with. This field is deprecated as of CUDA 6.0 and will always be NULL.\nL7472: \nL7473: Param buffer:\nL7474: \nL7475: \nL7476: The activity record buffer.\nL7477: \nL7478: Param size:\nL7479: \nL7480: \nL7481: The total size of the buffer in bytes as set in CUpti_BuffersCallbackRequestFunc.\nL7482: \nL7483: Param validSize:\nL7484: The number of valid bytes in the buffer.\nL7485: \nL7486: typedef void (*CUpti_BuffersCallbackCompleteFunc_v2)(uint8_t *buffer, size_t size, size_t validSize, 【1454†CUpti_BufferCallbackCompleteInfo】 *pBufferCompleteInfo)【1429†#】\nL7487: \nL7488: \nL7489: Function type for callback used by CUPTI to return a buffer of activity records.\nL7490: This callback function returns to the CUPTI client a buffer containing activity records. The buffer contains `validSize` bytes of activity records which should be read using cuptiActivityGetNextRecord. The number of dropped records can be read using cuptiActivityGetNumDroppedRecords. After this call CUPTI relinquished ownership of the buffer and will not use it anymore. The client may return the buffer to CUPTI using the CUpti_BuffersCallbackRequestFunc callback.\nL7491: Note: CUDA 6.0 onwards, all buffers returned by this callback are global buffers i.e. there is no context/stream specific buffer. User needs to parse the global buffer to extract the context/stream specific activity records.\nL7492: Param buffer:\nL7493: \nL7494: \nL7495: The activity record buffer.\nL7496: \nL7497: Param size:\nL7498: \nL7499: \nL7500: The total size of the buffer in bytes as set in CUpti_BuffersCallbackRequestFunc.\nL7501: \nL7502: Param validSize:\nL7503: \nL7504: \nL7505: The number of valid bytes in the buffer.\nL7506: \nL7507: Param pBufferCompleteInfo:\nL7508: \nL7509: \nL7510: Additional information about the completed buffer.\nL7511: \nL7512: typedef void (*CUpti_BuffersCallbackRequestFunc)(uint8_t **buffer, size_t *size, size_t *maxNumRecords)【1425†#】\nL7513: Function type for callback used by CUPTI to request an empty buffer for storing activity records.\nL7514: \nL7515: This callback function signals the CUPTI client that an activity buffer is needed by CUPTI. The activity buffer is used by CUPTI to store activity records. The callback function can decline the request by setting `*buffer` to NULL. In this case CUPTI may drop activity records.\nL7516: \nL7517: Param buffer:\nL7518: \nL7519: \nL7520: Returns the new buffer. If set to NULL then no buffer is returned.\nL7521: \nL7522: Param size:\nL7523: Returns the size of the returned buffer.\nL7524: \nL7525: Param maxNumRecords:\nL7526: \nL7527: \nL7528: Returns the maximum number of records that should be placed in the buffer. If 0 then the buffer is filled with as many records as possible. If > 0 the buffer is filled with at most that many records before it is returned.\nL7529: \nL7530: typedef void (*CUpti_BuffersCallbackRequestFunc_v2)(uint8_t **buffer, size_t *size, size_t *maxNumRecords, 【1455†CUpti_BufferCallbackRequestInfo】 *pBufferRequestInfo)【1428†#】\nL7531: Function type for callback used by CUPTI to request an empty buffer for storing activity records.\nL7532: \nL7533: This callback function signals the CUPTI client that an activity buffer is needed by CUPTI. The activity buffer is used by CUPTI to store activity records. The callback function can decline the request by setting `*buffer` to NULL. In this case CUPTI may drop activity records.\nL7534: \nL7535: Param buffer:\nL7536: \nL7537: \nL7538: Returns the new buffer. If set to NULL then no buffer is returned.\nL7539: \nL7540: Param size:\nL7541: Returns the size of the returned buffer.\nL7542: \nL7543: Param maxNumRecords:\nL7544: \nL7545: \nL7546: Returns the maximum number of records that should be placed in the buffer. If 0 then the buffer is filled with as many records as possible. If > 0 the buffer is filled with at most that many records before it is returned.\nL7547: \nL7548: Param pBufferRequestInfo:\nL7549: \nL7550: \nL7551: Additional information about the requested buffer.\nL7552: \nL7553: typedef uint64_t (*CUpti_TimestampCallbackFunc)(void)【1431†#】\nL7554: Function type for callback used by CUPTI to request a timestamp to be used in activity records.\nL7555: \nL7556: This callback function signals the CUPTI client that a timestamp needs to be returned. This timestamp would be treated as normalized timestamp to be used for various purposes in CUPTI. For example to store start and end timestamps reported in the CUPTI activity records. The returned timestamp must be in nanoseconds.\nL7557: \nL7558: See also\nL7559: \nL7560: 【443†cuptiActivityRegisterTimestampCallback】\nL7561: \nL7562: On this page\nL7563:   * 【482†6.1.7. Enumerations】\nL7564:     * 【493†`CUpti_ActivityAttribute`】\nL7565:       * 【508†`CUPTI_ACTIVITY_ATTR_DEVICE_BUFFER_PRE_ALLOCATE_VALUE`】\nL7566:       * 【509†`CUPTI_ACTIVITY_ATTR_PROFILING_SEMAPHORE_PRE_ALLOCATE_VALUE`】\nL7567:       * 【510†`CUPTI_ACTIVITY_ATTR_MEM_ALLOCATION_TYPE_HOST_PINNED`】\nL7568:       * 【512†`CUPTI_ACTIVITY_ATTR_DEVICE_BUFFER_SIZE_DEVICE_GRAPHS`】\nL7569:       * 【519†`CUPTI_ACTIVITY_ATTR_ENABLE_ALLOCATION_SOURCE_TRACKING`】\nL7570:       * 【520†`CUPTI_ACTIVITY_ATTR_ENABLE_KERNEL_LATENCY_TIMESTAMPS`】\nL7571:       * 【522†`CUPTI_ACTIVITY_ATTR_ENABLE_CUDA_EVENT_DEVICE_TIMESTAMPS`】\nL7572:       * 【523†`CUPTI_ACTIVITY_ATTR_ENABLE_KERNEL_LAUNCH_ATTRIBUTES`】\nL7573:       * 【525†`CUPTI_ACTIVITY_ATTR_ENABLE_MULTI_SUBSCRIBER_GRAPH_LEVEL_TRACE`】\nL7574:     * 【714†`CUpti_ActivityInstructionClass`】\nL7575:       * 【731†`CUPTI_ACTIVITY_INSTRUCTION_CLASS_INTER_THREAD_COMMUNICATION`】\nL7576:     * 【918†`CUpti_ActivityLaunchType`】\nL7577:       * 【920†`CUPTI_ACTIVITY_LAUNCH_TYPE_COOPERATIVE_SINGLE_DEVICE`】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2. Usage — Cupti (https://docs.nvidia.com/cupti/13.2.1/main/main.html)\nciteturn4view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view0\",\"pattern\":\"Last updated\"}); Total lines: 2978\nL2957: Trying to start a new CUPTI profiling session while another is active will trigger this error, so you should ensure that only one CUPTI-based tool accesses the profiling API at any time in your environment. This error can also occur when another NVIDIA developer tool such as Nsight Compute, CUDA-GDB, or Compute Sanitizer is already active.\nL2958:     * CUPTI_ERROR_NOT_INITIALIZED - CUPTI was unable to initialize its connection to the CUDA driver. This can occur if the CUDA driver version is not compatible with the installed CUPTI version, if CUPTI is missing, or if there are environment configuration issues.\nL2959:     * CUPTI_ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_PRIVILEGES - The application does not have sufficient privileges to perform the requested profiling operation. This often happens when the user lacks administrative rights or required capabilities (such as CAP_SYS_ADMIN) on the system.\nL2960: \nL2961:     * CUPTI_ERROR_HARDWARE_BUSY - The performance monitoring hardware is currently in use by another client, preventing CUPTI from accessing it for profiling.\nL2962:     * CUPTI_ERROR_INVALID_DEVICE - The specified device is not a valid CUDA device, which may be due to an incorrect device ID or a device not supported by CUPTI.\nL2963: \nL2964:   * Profiling may significantly change the overall performance characteristics of the application. Refer to the section 【388†CUPTI Overhead】 for more details.\nL2965: \nL2966:   * Keep your CUPTI version up to date to benefit from the latest features and bug fixes.\nL2967: \nL2968: On this page\nL2969:   * 【292†2.7. NVIDIA Tools Extension (NVTX) Support】\nL2970:     * 【297†2.7.3. Initialization to Receive NVTX Callbacks or Activity Records】\nL2971:     * 【301†2.7.4. CUPTI NVTX Extended Payload Support】\nL2972:       * 【303†2.7.4.2. CUPTI initialization for NVTX extended payload parsing】\nL2973: 【403†Privacy Policy†www.nvidia.com】 | 【404†Manage My Privacy†www.nvidia.com】 | 【405†Do Not Sell or Share My Data†www.nvidia.com】 | 【406†Terms of Service†www.nvidia.com】 | 【407†Accessibility†www.nvidia.com】 | 【408†Corporate Policies†www.nvidia.com】 | 【409†Product Security†www.nvidia.com】 | 【410†Contact†www.nvidia.com】\nL2974: \nL2975: Copyright © 2018-2026, NVIDIA Corporation 